 Fetches a web page and shows it, or saves it to a file.

 Format:
     CURL[/qualifiers] url
     CURL [-i] [-h] [-L] [-s] [-v] [-k] [-t|-b] [-x proxy] [-o file] url

 Put the URL in double quotes when its case matters:
     $ CURL/FOLLOW/OUTPUT=PAGE.HTM "http://example.com/Some/Path"

 A text/... response is saved as an ordinary RSTS text file; anything
 else as binary, with its exact length in the file's RMS attributes.
*Qualifiers
   /OUTPUT=file   -o file    save the body to a file, not show it
   /TEXT          -t         with /OUTPUT: save as text, any type
   /BINARY        -b         with /OUTPUT: save as binary
   /HEADERS       -i         show the response headers too
   /HEAD          -h         ask for the headers only
   /FOLLOW        -L         follow redirects (up to 5)
   /QUIET         -s         no progress or error chatter
   /VERBOSE       -v         show how the proxy's TLS went
   /INSECURE      -k         fetch even if the certificate fails
   /PROXY=host:port  -x      use this proxy (none: no proxy); else PROXY
                             in TCPIP.INI

 Qualifiers can be shortened to three letters (/OUT=, /FOL); /HEAD and
 /HEADERS are written out to the fifth letter (/HEADE).
*HTTPS
 RSTS has no TLS. CURL sends https:// requests in plain HTTP to
 sslproxy (sslproxy.py, on the MiSTer or any machine with Python 3),
 which fetches the page over TLS, checks the certificate and answers
 in plain HTTP. Set it once in TCPIP.INI:
     PROXY    192.168.1.10:3128
